  "title": "Week 1 (Oct 4): Greetings, calendar, alphabet, accents, pronouns",
  "markdown": "# Week 1 (Oct 4): Greetings, calendar, alphabet, accents, pronouns\n\nThis week sets up the basics: saying hello politely, the calendar words, how French letters sound, the 5 accents and the 8 subject pronouns.\n\n## 1. Greetings: formal (vous) or informal (tu)\n\n> **RULE** Use the **vous** forms with strangers, teachers, shops and offices. Use the **tu** forms with friends, family and children. If you are not sure, use vous.\n\n| Meaning | Formal (vous) | Informal (tu) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Please | S'il **vous** plaît | S'il **te** plaît |\n| Excuse me (to ask something) | Excus**ez**-moi | Excus**e**-moi |\n| Sorry (bumping into someone) | Pardonn**ez**-moi / Pardon | Pardonn**e**-moi / Pardon |\n| What's your name? | Comment vous appelez-vous ? | Comment tu t'appelles ? / Tu t'appelles comment ? |\n| How are you? | Comment allez-vous ? | Comment vas-tu ? / Ça va ? |\n| Let me introduce... | Je **vous** présente... | Je **te** présente... |\n\n> **RULE** Hello and goodbye change with the time of day. **Bonjour / Bonsoir** say hello. **Bonne journée / Bonne soirée / Bonne nuit** say goodbye.\n\n| Hello | Goodbye | See you... |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Bonjour (daytime) | Bonne journée | À tout à l'heure (later today) |\n| Bonsoir (evening) | Bonne soirée | À ce soir (this evening) |\n| Salut ! (friends, hi or bye) | Bonne nuit (going to bed) | À demain (tomorrow) |\n|  | Au revoir | À bientôt (soon) / À plus tard (later) |\n\nOther must-know words: Merci (beaucoup), De rien, Enchanté(e), Félicitations, Bonne chance, Bienvenue, Allô (on the phone only).\n\nReplying to \"Ça va ?\": Oui, ça va (bien). / Pas mal ! / Ça ne va pas.\n\n## 2. Days, months, times of day\n\n> **RULE** Days and months are **not capitalised** in French: lundi, novembre. Write the date as day + number + month: **samedi 14 novembre**.\n\n- Days: lundi, mardi, mercredi, jeudi, vendredi, samedi, dimanche\n- Months: janvier, février, mars, avril, mai, juin, juillet, août, septembre, octobre, novembre, décembre\n- Times of day: le matin (morning), l'après-midi (afternoon), le soir (evening), la nuit (night)\n- \"This...\": **ce** matin, **cet** après-midi, **ce** soir, **cette** nuit\n\n## 3. The letters that sound different\n\n> **RULE** **G** sounds like \"zh\" before e, i, y (girafe, garage) and like \"g\" elsewhere (gâteau). **J** is always \"zh\" (je, jour). **H** is silent (hôtel, homme). **R** comes from the throat (rue, rouge, Paris).\n\n> **RULE** Final consonants are usually silent: Paris, vous, parlent. In a **liaison**, the silent final s turns into a \"z\" sound when the next word starts with a vowel or silent h.\n\n| Written | Say it | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| nous parlons | noo par-lon | p is a consonant: no liaison |\n| nous achetons | noo-z-ach-ton | vowel next: liaison |\n| vous avez | voo-z-avay | vowel next: liaison |\n| ils ont | eel-z-on | vowel next: liaison |\n| elles parlent | el parl | -ent of verbs is silent |\n\nVowels in French: a, e, i, o, u, y. A silent **h** behaves like a vowel (j'habite, l'hôtel).\n\n## 4. The 5 accents\n\n| Accent | On | What it does | Examples |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| aigu ´ | é only | \"ay\" sound, as in \"day\" | bébé, épice, parlé (spoke) vs parle (speak) |\n| grave ` | è, à, ù | è = open \"eh\"; on à and ù it separates two words | père, frère, après; là (there) vs la (the); où (where) vs ou (or) |\n| cédille ¸ | ç only | makes c sound like s before a, o, u | garçon, français, leçon, ça va |\n| circonflexe ^ | â ê î ô û | often marks an old s; check the English cousin | hôpital (hospital), forêt (forest), fête (feast), île (isle) |\n| tréma ¨ | ë ï ü | say the two vowels separately | Noël, maïs (corn) vs mais (but), naïf |\n\n> **RULE** An accent can change the meaning: **où** = where, **ou** = or; **là** = there, **la** = the; **sûr** = sure, **sur** = on; **tâche** = task, **tache** = stain.\n\n> **RULE** C before e or i already sounds like s (Francine, merci). It needs **ç** only before a, o, u: commençons, garçon, ça.\n\n## 5. Subject pronouns\n\n| French | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| je / j' | I (j' before a vowel or h) |\n| tu | you (one person, informal) |\n| il / elle / on | he / she / one, \"we\" in speech |\n| nous | we |\n| vous | you (formal, or more than one person) |\n| ils | they (all boys, or a mixed group) |\n| elles | they (all girls) |\n\n> **WATCH OUT** One boy in a group of girls makes the whole group **ils**.\n\n## Fixes to the class notes\n\n- \"Les moins de l'année\" → **Les mois de l'année** (moins = less).\n- L'après-midi = **the afternoon**, not \"the noon\".\n- é sounds like \"ay\" (écouter = ay-koo-tay), not \"ee\".\n- mangé = **eaten** (past participle), not \"ate\".\n- Bienvenu → **Bienvenue !** when you welcome someone.\n- Une âme = a **soul**; un état = a **state**; le vêtement = a **piece of clothing**.\n- Hôpital always has the ô: **hôpital**.",
